<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacobite rising of 1715</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1715">1715</a>–<a href="/years/1716">1716</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Fifteen, Jacobite rebellion of 1715</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jacobite rising of 1715 was an attempt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-francis-edward-stuart">James Francis Edward Stuart</a>, the Old Pretender, to take the British throne from George I. The Earl of Mar raised the Jacobite standard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, but the indecisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sheriffmuir">Battle of Sheriffmuir</a> and the surrender at Preston in November 1715 ended its momentum. James arrived too late and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in February 1716.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1715.jpg" alt="Jacobite rising of 1715" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Wootton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Sherrifmuir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1688 replaced the Catholic James II and VII with his Protestant daughter Mary II and her husband William III, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement 1701</a> excluded Catholics, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-francis-edward-stuart">James Francis Edward Stuart</a>, from the throne. When Anne died in August 1714 the crown passed smoothly to George I, and the pro-Hanoverian Whigs took control of government. They turned on the Tories: Robert Harley was imprisoned in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and Lord Bolingbroke fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and became James's Secretary of State.</p><p>French support had been crucial to the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-stuart">Stuarts</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had accepted the Protestant succession in the peace that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. On 14 March 1715 James appealed to Pope Clement XI for help, and on 23 August, believing that Marlborough would join him, he wrote to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-fitzjames-1st-duke-of-berwick">Duke of Berwick</a> that it was now more than ever now or never.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Earl of Mar acted without a commission from James. On 6 September at Braemar he raised the standard of James the 8th and 3rd before 600 supporters. The Jacobites took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a>, Gordon Castle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> and Dundee but not Fort William. By October Mar's force of nearly 20,000 held all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a> except Stirling Castle, but his hesitation gave the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> time to gather reinforcements.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the government arrested the leaders of a planned western rising on the night of 2 October. A rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> under James Radclyffe, 3rd Earl of Derwentwater, went ahead on 6 October, joined a force of Scottish Borderer Jacobites led by William Gordon, 6th Viscount Kenmure, and the men of Mackintosh of Borlum, and surrendered after the Battle of Preston on 12–14 November. Under the Barrier Treaty the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sent 6,000 troops to defend the Protestant succession.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>James landed at Peterhead on 22 December, but by the time he reached Perth on 9 January 1716 his army numbered fewer than 5,000. Mar burned villages between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> to deny <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> supplies and led the Jacobites out of Perth on 30 January; on 4 February James wrote a farewell letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and sailed from Montrose the next day.</p><p>Many prisoners were tried for treason and sentenced to death. The Indemnity Act of July 1717 pardoned all who had taken part, except the whole of Clan Gregor, Rob Roy MacGregor among them. James tried again in 1719 and was defeated at the Battle of Glenshiel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacobite rising of 1715” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Fifteen</b> runners set out on the first Boston race, inspired by the marathon at the 1896 Athens Olympics.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-19">19 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Fifteen</b> independent states took its place.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-31">31 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Neither side won clearly, but Mar withdrew and the <b>Jacobite rising of 1715</b> lost its momentum.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-13">13 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
